WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.771
If you want to hear about how one dark story
point cut from the movie is hidden in the

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:06.100
background of this movie, then stick around
to the end of this video.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:10.480
[INTRO]

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:11.860
Welcome back to Things You Missed.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:15.440
Today I'll be covering one of my favorite
psychological horror films that kind of flew

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:19.640
under the radar because of the sheer volume
of great horror movies that came out in 2016.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.130
I'm talking about The Boy.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:24.570
This is one of those movies that can be fun
look back on because it is only after learning

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:27.150
of the twist that some of the Things You Missed
start to make sense.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.520
So as usual with these types of movies, I'll
urge you to make sure you've seen the movie

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:34.630
before hearing me analyse it, so with that
in mind, let's get into the Things You Missed.

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:36.800
[THINGS YOU MISSED]

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.440
When Greta arrives at the house for the first
time, she is still asleep in the car, and

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.720
the driver, after bringing in her stuff, has
to wake her up.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.220
"Apologies Miss.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.700
I didn't know how else to wake you."

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.570
This first interaction she has is symbolic
of the time she would spend there, most of which

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:56.510
is her being frightened by the man on the
other side of the mirror.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:00.410
She finds that the house is seemingly empty
until she meets the grocery delivery man Malcolm

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:02.050
and he shows her around the kitchen.

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:05.550
The crates he uses to deliver the food are
from Broadhurst Farm Grocers.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:09.470
Broadhurst is one of the many examples of
the initials BH, which come up again and again

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:10.470
in this story.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.260
The boy she is hired to look after, Brahms
Heelshire, has the initials BH; the letters

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:18.410
are carved into the rat traps set up around
the house; and the book that Greta later reads

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:21.780
out loud to Brahms has a character named Mr.
Brocklehurst, BH.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:22.950
More on that later.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.240
She meets the family and they explain the
rules, like how she is to play music at an

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:30.080
uncomfortably loud volume, most likely so
that the real Brahms can have some time to

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:31.950
move freely, possibly to exercise.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:35.640
She is also to clear out the rat traps daily,
because Mrs. Heelshire is afraid of the rodents

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.140
getting into the walls where he's living.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.911
It also gives a plausible reason as to why
she is to discard any uneaten food in the

00:01:40.911 --> 00:01:44.060
sealed chest, it gives Brahms the opportunity
to feast on her leftovers.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:45.940
"Our son is here.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.420
He's very much with us."

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:49.960
Well, at least he gets points for honesty.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:53.340
I also find it interesting that he tells her
this stuff while they're outside, because

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.540
the rooms inside the house seem to be all
connected by these tubes, which carry the

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:57.540
sound.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.010
"And that was it.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:03.960
Which I suppose is all the answer we're gonna
get."

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.561
This system is perfect for Brahms to keep
tabs on her, he can listen in on her and make

00:02:07.561 --> 00:02:10.950
sure she’s following the rules no matter
where he is in the house, while also making

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:14.950
noises to try to convince her that the spirit
of Brahms is present in the house.

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:18.400
To cover this up, the Heelshire’s have added
a rule that she can’t have any guests over,

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:22.200
and only Malcolm can come in to make the deliveries,
this way she never becomes aware of the way

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.810
the sound carries because for the most part
she’s the only one making any sounds.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:29.230
If you were wondering if the Heelshires are
in on it, or if they too were being fooled

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:30.840
by Brahms, consider this.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.060
Before they leave, they take a moment alone,
which I’m guessing was for a final face

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:35.380
to face with the real Brahms.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.900
“Could you give us a moment alone to speak
to Brahms privately?”

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:40.900
“Oh sure.”

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:45.700
I’ll crank up the volume right here, where
you can actually hear Brahms’s voice.

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:46.709
"Brahms".

00:02:46.709 --> 00:02:51.660
*unintelligible*

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.580
"I think she's lovely.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:55.460
*unintelligible*

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:57.780
"I think she should stay."

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.239
You can’t make out what he’s saying, but
you can tell that it isn’t Mr. or Mrs. Heelshire.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.400
“He wants you, Ms. Evans.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:06.459
He’s chosen you if you’ll have him.”

00:03:06.459 --> 00:03:09.570
The backstory that we later learn tells us
that Brahms used to play with a young girl

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:11.989
named Emily, but one day she was found killed
in the woods.

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:15.840
It was most likely Brahms, who had always
had some issues, so The Heelshires started

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.760
a fire and faked their son’s death to avoid
the consequences.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.840
They let him hide for years inside the walls,
until they can’t do it anymore, so that’s

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.160
why they pretend they are going on holiday,
where they really just end up offing themselves

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.630
out of guilt for what they allowed Brahms
to become.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:32.890
They also seem nervous as they’re leaving,
probably because Brahms has become too powerful

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:35.560
and they don’t know if their vacation cover
story is gonna hold up.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:40.520
I think Brahms grave marker is another clue,
it says ‘he shall not perish but have everlasting

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:43.770
life’, though I’m not sure why they would
leave a clue to a secret they’re trying

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:44.760
to keep.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.160
"I'm so sorry..."

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:49.280
"Come along my dear.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:50.930
It's time we left."

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:53.569
Guess they just wanted a little inside joke
between the two of them.

00:03:53.569 --> 00:03:57.430
The penultimate rule is “Brahms is never
to leave,” because looking back on it, the

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:00.560
illusion that the doll is possessed would
never hold up in the outside world, it only

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.319
works because the real Brahms is able to emerge
from inside the walls while Greta isn’t

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:05.319
looking.

00:04:05.319 --> 00:04:08.440
So all of these tactics are enough to plant
that subconscious seed in Greta’s mind that

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:10.200
Brahms could really be alive.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.410
In her first dream scene, the shot begins
with us looking through a mirror, and it is

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:16.540
through the mirror where Brahms would eventually
reveal himself.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:19.939
There are many shots like this that seems
to be coming from Brahm’s point of view,

00:04:19.939 --> 00:04:23.279
whether he’s stealing her stuff while she's
in the shower, spying on her through a keyhole

00:04:23.279 --> 00:04:24.279
or otherwise.

00:04:24.279 --> 00:04:28.539
The dream ends with a hand reaching out through
the wall to attack her, so perhaps some subconscious

00:04:28.539 --> 00:04:31.440
part of her suspected the truth as a possibility.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.270
After things start to get weird around the
house, Greta gives in and starts following

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:37.590
the routine that the Heelshire’s had laid
out for her, which includes reading aloud

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:38.759
to the doll every day.

00:04:38.759 --> 00:04:42.270
She reads a selection from Jane Eyre, a 19th
Century British novel.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:46.189
There are a ton of parallels between the story
of Jane Eyre and Greta’s story in The Boy,

00:04:46.189 --> 00:04:49.979
but the most notable part is when Jane becomes
a Governess, which is essentially the role

00:04:49.979 --> 00:04:53.029
Greta is filling at the house, and meets a
man Rochester.

00:04:53.029 --> 00:04:56.580
One evening Jane discovers that Rochester
has been set on fire in his sleep and it turns

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.949
out to be a madwoman in the attic who was
responsible.

00:04:58.949 --> 00:05:02.840
Jane saves Rochester’s life, just as Greta
goes back to save Malcolm from Brahms in The

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:03.840
Boy.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.469
If you’ve read Jane Eyre, I’m sure you
can find other parallels as well, but I’m

00:05:06.469 --> 00:05:10.189
going to move on to the dark truth behind
Brahms’s intentions in the movie.

00:05:10.189 --> 00:05:13.589
In a Q&amp;A with the screenwriter Stacey Menear,
he reveals that Greta wasn’t brought in

00:05:13.589 --> 00:05:17.699
to be either a girlfriend or mother figure
to Brahms, but rather a messed up in-between

00:05:17.699 --> 00:05:18.699
of the two.

00:05:18.699 --> 00:05:21.779
We get a pretty good idea of this when they
discover the real room, with the the makeshift

00:05:21.779 --> 00:05:25.680
sack-doll wearing her dress on the bed, but
what you may not have noticed is the used

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:26.680
tissues.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:28.440
I’ll let you draw your own conclusion with
that.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.389
The doll also has a full head of hair.

00:05:30.389 --> 00:05:33.610
It’s possible that at least some of that
is her real hair, because we see her notice

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:36.780
a chopped portion upon getting out of the
shower earlier in the film.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:40.659
The arms are covered in what seem to be refrigerator
letter magnets, maybe the director’s clue

00:05:40.659 --> 00:05:43.440
about the in-between status that Menear was
talking about.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:45.529
That brings us to the last rule: kiss goodnight.

00:05:45.529 --> 00:05:49.680
I can only imagine, given everything that’s
been presented that Brahms did not plan on

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:50.690
stopping at the kiss.

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:53.100
It certainly gives new meaning to this line.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:58.479
“Well we’ve had a number of potential
nannies come through already.

00:05:58.479 --> 00:06:00.460
Brahms has rejected them all.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:05.150
Though they weren’t nearly as young or as
pretty as you.”

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:10.130
It was also hinted at that Brahms exhibited
a sort of sadistic behavior as a child, many

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:14.499
rumors in town seem to think he was responsible
for crushing the young girl Emily’s skull,

00:06:14.499 --> 00:06:18.580
and that does seem possible after seeing the
superhuman strength displayed during his rampage.

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:22.919
He also has an array of animal traps and possible
torture devices hanging from his ceiling.

00:06:22.919 --> 00:06:26.649
I think that means the BH carved into the
rat trap that I mentioned earlier probably

00:06:26.649 --> 00:06:30.810
doesn’t stand for Brahms, and sadism is
one of the many psychological issues that

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:31.810
he has.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:34.699
Where else would he have gotten the blood
used to leave the message for Greta’s ex-boyfriend

00:06:34.699 --> 00:06:35.699
Cole?

00:06:35.699 --> 00:06:38.559
There are plenty of secrets hidden all over
this movie, so let me know what you might

00:06:38.559 --> 00:06:42.080
have noticed in the comments, and if you love
horror, don’t hide behind that mirror, come

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:46.779
out and subscribe to CZsWorld for new horrors
every week, ring that deathbell for notifications

00:06:46.779 --> 00:06:48.969
and I’ll see you in the next one.

00:06:48.969 --> 00:06:51.270
Assuming we both survive.

